1
00:00:00,033 --> 00:00:04,833
Let a shape smooth itself out, the way heat spreads.

2
00:00:04,833 --> 00:00:12,633
This is Ricci flow, the tool behind the proof of the Poincaré conjecture.

3
00:00:12,633 --> 00:00:19,933
Sometimes the flow tears: a neck pinches, and the curvature there blows up.

4
00:00:19,933 --> 00:00:24,700
Can it tear while the simplest curvature reading stays calm?

5
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:37,033
OpenAI claims an answer: no in four dimensions, yes in high dimensions.

6
00:00:40,266 --> 00:00:53,100
Ricci flow, introduced by Richard Hamilton in 1982, changes how lengths are measured over time, driven by the Ricci curvature.

7
00:00:53,100 --> 00:01:03,733
A round sphere simply shrinks. Its curvature grows until, at a finite time, the sphere disappears.

8
00:01:03,733 --> 00:01:07,300
A flow can also break down at a thin neck.

9
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:13,833
Perelman understood these breakdowns in three dimensions well enough to cut them out and continue,

10
00:01:13,833 --> 00:01:18,666
and that proved the Poincaré conjecture.

11
00:01:18,666 --> 00:01:26,866
So: Ricci flow smooths shapes, but it can break down.

12
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:39,500
At each point, the full curvature is a collection of numbers: how the space bends in every pair of directions.

13
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:49,534
Averaging gives the Ricci curvature, and averaging again gives a single number, the scalar curvature.

14
00:01:49,534 --> 00:01:55,434
In four dimensions, the full curvature has twenty numbers at each point,

15
00:01:55,434 --> 00:02:02,634
the Ricci curvature ten, and the scalar curvature just one.

16
00:02:02,634 --> 00:02:09,567
So a space can have zero scalar curvature, and still be curved.

17
00:02:09,567 --> 00:02:17,734
So scalar curvature is a single number, and it can miss a lot.

18
00:02:21,966 --> 00:02:26,800
A flow continues as long as its full curvature stays bounded.

19
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:32,633
In 2005 Natasha Šešum showed bounded Ricci curvature is enough.

20
00:02:32,633 --> 00:02:39,133
The question is whether bounded scalar curvature is enough.

21
00:02:39,133 --> 00:02:44,866
The danger is a tiny piece with zero Ricci curvature that keeps shrinking.

22
00:02:44,866 --> 00:02:52,933
Its scalar curvature stays zero, yet its full curvature grows without bound.

23
00:02:52,933 --> 00:03:03,566
Earlier work showed that any such tear would have to be fast: faster than the usual rate at which spheres and necks collapse.

24
00:03:03,566 --> 00:03:11,866
So the question is: is bounded scalar curvature enough to keep going?

25
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:20,434
The first paper claims yes, in four dimensions.

26
00:03:20,434 --> 00:03:29,067
If the scalar curvature stays bounded, the flow continues smoothly on the same space.

27
00:03:29,067 --> 00:03:38,967
To prove it, zoom into a would-be tear. You find a tree of pieces with zero Ricci curvature, nested inside each other.

28
00:03:38,967 --> 00:03:47,667
An energy, the total scalar curvature with a correction at the edge, shows the outermost piece cannot keep shrinking.

29
00:03:47,667 --> 00:03:52,300
So the tear never forms.

30
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:56,567
The second paper claims no, in high dimensions.

31
00:03:56,567 --> 00:04:07,467
It builds a flow, in at least thirteen dimensions, whose scalar curvature stays bounded while the shape still tears.

32
00:04:07,467 --> 00:04:12,300
The shape is very symmetric, described by two radii.

33
00:04:12,300 --> 00:04:21,067
Near one end it collapses toward a cone, with curvature blowing up as fast as one likes.

34
00:04:21,067 --> 00:04:30,267
So the claims are: yes in four dimensions, no in high dimensions.

35
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:40,834
Neither paper has a computer-checked proof, and we found no expert assessment yet.

36
00:04:40,834 --> 00:04:48,867
The four-dimensional proof also relies on a companion paper from the same release.

37
00:04:48,867 --> 00:04:54,767
The Poincaré conjecture itself is already proved, and is not in question.

38
00:04:54,767 --> 00:05:02,234
These papers are about its tool, in four dimensions and beyond.

39
00:05:02,234 --> 00:05:11,400
But if they hold, they settle a basic question about how Ricci flow can break down.
